\f0\fs24 \cf0 Farport Privacy Policy / \uc0\u1055 \u1086 \u1083 \u1080 \u1090 \u1080 \u1082 \u1072  \u1082 \u1086 \u1085 \u1092 \u1080 \u1076 \u1077 \u1085 \u1094 \u1080 \u1072 \u1083 \u1100 \u1085 \u1086 \u1089 \u1090 \u1080  Farport\
\
Effective date / \uc0\u1044 \u1072 \u1090 \u1072  \u1074 \u1089 \u1090 \u1091 \u1087 \u1083 \u1077 \u1085 \u1080 \u1103  \u1074  \u1089 \u1080 \u1083 \u1091 : 2026-11-02\
\
English\
\
About this policy\
\
Farport is an SSH client for Mac, iPhone and iPad, with SFTP and Finder integration on Mac. It is developed by Andrei Pantiukhin (the developer). This policy describes how Farport handles information when you use the app or contact the developer. You do not need to register a Farport account.\
\
The developer does not operate a backend that receives your SSH sessions, server credentials or transferred files. The app processes information on your device, connects to servers you configure and, if you enable synchronization, uses Apple's iCloud service as described below. The developer does not sell your personal information.\
\
Information processed on your device\
\
Farport uses the information you provide to deliver its features:\
\
- Host profiles: server names and addresses, ports, usernames, authentication types, initial directories, notes, jump-host routes and session preferences.\
- Workspace and settings: terminal tab identifiers, names and order, selected host and tab, interface preferences and connection-related state.\
- Credentials and server trust: passwords, imported private keys, key passphrases and trusted server-key fingerprints. These are stored using Apple's Keychain; credentials are also held in memory when the app needs them for connections.\
- Terminal content: commands, other input and output from your remote sessions. Local terminal history is held in memory while the terminal is retained; Farport does not save it as a transcript across app restarts.\
- Files on Mac: remote file names, paths and metadata, files you choose to upload or download, and Finder connection information. Finder integration can retain downloaded files and metadata in system-managed local caches, including after Farport closes. Opening a file in another app gives that app access to the file.\
\
Host profiles, notes, workspace metadata and synchronization records are stored in the app's local data files. Farport does not separately encrypt these local metadata files. Do not put passwords or private keys in host names, tab names or notes.\
\
Connections to your servers\
\
When you connect, Farport sends the information needed to authenticate and carry out your actions to the server you selected. This can include your username, password authentication, commands, terminal input and uploaded files. Private-key authentication uses the key on your device; Farport does not upload the private key or its passphrase to the server.\
\
Connections use SSH, including SFTP file transfers. If you configure ProxyJump, connections pass through your selected gateway servers. Servers and gateways can observe connection information such as your IP address, and their operators may retain logs or remote content according to their own policies and configuration. The developer does not control those servers, their storage or their privacy practices.\
\
Farport requests local-network access when needed to reach servers on your local network. You can control this permission in your device's system settings. On Mac, file import, export and Finder integration also use the access you grant through the operating system.\
\
Optional iCloud synchronization\
\
iCloud synchronization is off until you enable it. When enabled, Farport stores host profiles and terminal tab definitions in a private CloudKit database associated with your Apple Account, so your devices can synchronize them. This includes host identifiers, names, addresses, ports, usernames, authentication types, notes, initial directories, jump-host references, session preferences and tab identifiers, names and order. Synchronization also uses record identifiers, revision information and deletion markers to reconcile changes.\
\
Passwords, private keys, key passphrases, trusted server-key fingerprints, terminal input/output, transferred file contents and Finder locations are not included in Farport's iCloud host synchronization. Credentials and trust must be configured separately on each device.\
\
Farport uses CloudKit's encrypted record fields for profile content. Apple operates iCloud and handles the service under its own privacy policy (https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/). The developer does not receive a copy of your private CloudKit records through a developer-operated service. This is not a promise of a separate, app-managed end-to-end encryption system.\
\
Disabling synchronization stops further app synchronization but keeps existing local and iCloud data. Changing your Apple Account does not erase the local host library. If you enable synchronization with another account, the local profiles can be uploaded to that account after the app's confirmation.\
\
Diagnostics, advertising and tracking\
\
Farport does not include advertising, third-party behavioral analytics, tracking SDKs or a developer-operated crash-reporting service. The app does not use advertising identifiers or advertising cookies.\
\
Connection errors and status messages are processed locally and may include server addresses, usernames, paths or protocol details. They are not automatically sent to the developer by Farport. Apple may process or share app diagnostics and usage information under its platform services and the sharing choices available in your system settings. See Apple's privacy policy (https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/).\
\
Support correspondence\
\
If you email the developer, the developer receives your email address, your message and any files, screenshots or diagnostics you choose to attach. This information is used to respond to your request and investigate the issue. Email providers process the correspondence to deliver and store it. Do not include passwords, private keys or confidential terminal output in support messages.\
\
Support correspondence is kept while needed to handle your request and related follow-up, or to meet applicable obligations. You may request access, correction or deletion of information you sent to support using the contact address below.\
\
Retention, deletion and your choices\
\
You can edit or delete saved hosts, close terminal tabs, forget trusted server keys, disconnect Finder locations and turn off iCloud synchronization in Farport.\
\
Deleting a host removes it from the active local host library. Associated app-owned credential references and Keychain items are retired as part of the app's cleanup; separately retained Finder data, caches or backups may remain. If synchronization is enabled, the host deletion is queued for propagation to iCloud and your other devices when synchronization succeeds.\
\
The current version retains synchronization deletion markers and record identifiers to prevent deleted entries from returning. It can also retain local recovered versions of conflicting profiles, which you can dismiss separately. Deleting a host or disabling synchronization is not a complete erasure of all synchronization metadata. Farport does not currently provide an in-app command to erase the entire private CloudKit database.\
\
Downloaded or exported files, system-managed Finder caches, Keychain items and backups are not necessarily all removed by uninstalling the app. Use the relevant system, file-management and backup controls to manage those copies. Deleting a host or disconnecting does not delete files, logs or tmux sessions on a remote server; manage those on that server.\
\
The developer cannot retrieve your device's local app data, Keychain secrets or private CloudKit records through a Farport backend. Contact the developer for questions about the app's data controls or information you supplied directly to support.\
\
Security\
\
Farport uses SSH for server connections, Keychain for credentials and explicit server-key verification. You should verify server fingerprints, protect your devices and Apple Account, and use only servers you trust. No method of electronic storage or network communication can guarantee absolute security.\
\
Children and external services\
\
Farport is a general-purpose developer tool and is not designed specifically for children. The developer does not knowingly request personal information from children. If you believe a child has sent personal information to support, contact the developer to request its removal.\
\
Remote servers, Apple services, other apps used to open files and external websites have their own privacy practices. Review the policies of the services you choose to use with Farport.\
\
Changes and contact\
\
This policy may be updated when the app or its data practices change. An updated policy will show a new effective date on this page.\
\
For privacy questions or requests, contact Andrei Pantiukhin at panandafog@icloud.com.\
